Is it possible to use Z3 as the solver (I believe Spacer is the underlying CHC engine)? If the challenge is identifying the $I_1$ and $I_2$ for sygus problems, https://github.com/PL-ML/code2inv seems to provide a CHC frontend and manually translated the problems to C. If time is a constraint, a sample of 10 problems from the code2inv benchmark will provide some confidence and the full evaluation can be added in the final manuscript.

Example: 
```
int main() {
  // variable declarations
  int n;
  int x;
  // pre-conditions
  (x = 0);
  // loop body
  while ((x < n)) {
    {
    (x  = (x + 1));
    }

  }
  // post-condition
if ( (n >= 0) )
assert( (x == n) );

}
```

The dependency on a CHC solver "As for CHC solvers, to our knowledge, none of the standard tools use interpolation directly for these tasks." makes me worried that this method is limited in applicability.

final_justification.value:
I am very concerned that the results do not generalize outside of the narrow settings tested in the paper. In particular, the ask was made to test on at least 10 problems in the standard LIA-Inv_Track from sygus. The loop invariant inference problems are exactly in the flavor that the authors motivate their paper and no more difficult than 
```x = 0; y = 0;
while(*) {x++; y+=2}
assert(x=100=> y=200);```

problem they highlighted in the rebuttal.

I think the authors should consider either supporting array theories or be applied to LIA problems in a downstream application to strengthen the paper.

